.NET 8+ (including .NET 10) ships with a built-in abstraction for this: **`System.TimeProvider`**.

**In your production code:**

```csharp
public class ReportGenerator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _timeProvider;

    public ReportGenerator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
    {
        _timeProvider = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;
    }

    public Report Generate()
    {
        var now = _timeProvider.GetUtcNow(); // instead of DateTime.UtcNow
        // ...
    }
}
```

**In your tests**, install the testing package:

```bash
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

Then use `FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing;

var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 9, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var generator = new ReportGenerator(fake);
var report = generator.Generate();

Assert.Equal(new DateTime(2026, 1, 15, 9, 0, 0, DateTimeKind.Utc), report.GeneratedAt);

// You can also advance time:
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromHours(1));
```

No need to create your own `IClock` interface — `TimeProvider` is the framework-supported solution and works with DI, `ITimer`, and other time-dependent APIs.
